    <p class="lede first reveal">Ask most people what a brand is and they'll point at the surface: the logo, the colours, the font that felt right at two in the morning. So they spend a weekend in a design tool, land on a palette, and wonder why the business still doesn't feel like anything. They branded the wrapping and skipped the thing inside.</p>
    <p class="reveal">Here is the part no one puts on the moodboard: a brand is a promise, kept so consistently that people start to expect it. It's the feeling someone gets when your content shows up — the sense of who this is for and what it reliably delivers. The palette is downstream of that. It's the outfit, not the person.</p>
    <p class="reveal"><strong>You do not have a design problem. You have a definition problem.</strong> Once you can say what you promise, to whom, and how it should feel, the colours and fonts get easy — because now they have a job. Skip the definition, and no amount of prettiness will save it.</p>

    <div class="plate-photo reveal">
